## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design runtime-extendable components using a simple plugin registry, mirroring real-world maintenance and extension needs.

 ## Key Concepts

- Plugin interface and duck typing
- Runtime registry and manifest loading
- Execution order and error handling
- Testing plugin isolation

 ## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal plugin interface via duck typing
class Plugin {
  process(line) { return line; }
}

class RedactPlugin { process(line) { return line.replace(/password=\S+/g, 'password=REDACTED'); } }
class TimestampPlugin { process(line) { return `[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${line}`; } }

class Registry {
  constructor() { this.factories = new Map(); }
  register(name, factory) { this.factories.set(name, factory); }
  create(name) { const f = this.factories.get(name); return f ? f() : null; }
}

// Boot: register plugins
const registry = new Registry();
registry.register('redact', () => new RedactPlugin());
registry.register('timestamp', () => new TimestampPlugin());

// Manifest-driven sequence
const manifest = ['redact', 'timestamp'];
let line = 'user=alice password=hunter';
for (const name of manifest) {
  const plugin = registry.create(name);
  if (plugin) line = plugin.process(line);
}
console.log(line);
```

 ## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test plugin isolation and ensure a misbehaving plugin doesn't crash the pipeline?
- How would you support dynamic reloading of the manifest without restarting the process?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a small, extensible plugin system for logging using runtime registration and fault isolation.

## Key Concepts
- **Open-Closed Principle**: The Logger remains open for extension but closed for modification
- **Strategy/Plugin Patterns**: Each backend implements a common interface and can be swapped or added at runtime
- **Registry Pattern**: Enables runtime backend discovery and management by name
- **Asynchronous Dispatch**: Non-blocking logging ensures performance isn't impacted by slow backends
- **Fault Isolation**: A failing backend shouldn't crash the entire application

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Backend {
  name: string;
  log(level: LogLevel, msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): void | Promise<void>;
}

type LogLevel = 'debug' | 'info' | 'warn' | 'error';

class BackendRegistry {
  private backends = new Map<string, Backend>();

  register(name: string, backend: Backend): void {
    this.backends.set(name, backend);
  }

  get(name: string): Backend | undefined {
    return this.backends.get(name);
  }

  unregister(name: string): boolean {
    return this.backends.delete(name);
  }
}

class Logger {
  constructor(private registry: BackendRegistry) {}

  async log(level: LogLevel, msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    const backends = Array.from(this.registry['backends'].values());
    await Promise.allSettled(
      backends.map(backend =>
        Promise.resolve(backend.log(level, msg, meta)).catch(() => {})
      )
    );
  }

  debug(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('debug', msg, meta);
  }

  info(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('info', msg, meta);
  }

  warn(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('warn', msg, meta);
  }

  error(msg: string, meta?: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<void> {
    return this.log('error', msg, meta);
  }
}

// Usage Example
const registry = new BackendRegistry();

const consoleBackend: Backend = {
  name: 'console',
  log(level, msg, meta) {
    console.log(`[${level}] ${msg}`, meta || '');
  },
};

const fileBackend: Backend = {
  name: 'file',
  async log(level, msg, meta) {
    // Write to file implementation
  },
};

registry.register('console', consoleBackend);
registry.register('file', fileBackend);

const logger = new Logger(registry);
logger.info('Application started', { version: '1.0.0' });
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the open-closed principle, runtime extensibility, and clean separation between flag evaluation and rollout strategies. It also evaluates the ability to design for multiple concurrent experiments with real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts

- **Strategy Pattern**: Enables extensible rollout logic where new strategies can be added without modifying existing code
- **Runtime Registry**: Provides plugin-like extensibility, allowing new strategies to be registered dynamically
- **Thread-Safe Lazy Initialization**: Ensures safe concurrent access and defers expensive computations until needed
- **Minimal TypeScript Interfaces**: Small surface area reduces complexity and maintenance burden
- **Context-Based Evaluation**: Makes decisions using user attributes, device information, and geographic data

## Code Example

```typescript
interface FlagContext {
  userId?: string;
  device?: string;
  region?: string;
  attributes?: Record<string, unknown>;
}

interface RolloutStrategy {
  name: string;
  evaluate(context: FlagContext, config: RolloutConfig): boolean;
}

interface RolloutConfig {
  percentage?: number;
  segment?: string;
  shardKey?: string;
}

class StrategyRegistry {
  private strategies = new Map<string, () => RolloutStrategy>();

  register(name: string, factory: () => RolloutStrategy): void {
    this.strategies.set(name, factory);
  }

  get(name: string): RolloutStrategy {
    const factory = this.strategies.get(name);
    if (!factory) throw new Error(`Strategy '${name}' not found`);
    return factory();
  }
}

class FeatureFlagEngine {
  private registry: StrategyRegistry;
  private strategyCache = new Map<string, RolloutStrategy>();

  constructor(registry: StrategyRegistry) {
    this.registry = registry;
  }

  evaluate(flag: string, context: FlagContext, config: RolloutConfig): boolean {
    const strategy = this.getStrategy(config.strategy);
    return strategy.evaluate(context, config);
  }

  private getStrategy(name: string): RolloutStrategy {
    if (!this.strategyCache.has(name)) {
      this.strategyCache.set(name, this.registry.get(name));
    }
    return this.strategyCache.get(name)!;
  }
}
```

