## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a runtime-extendable pipeline, ensuring immutability, async handling, and per-transform error reporting. It also exercises runtime registration and ordering without redeploys.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern and dynamic lookup
- Pipeline composition with explicit order
- Immutability and async error handling
- Observability of per-transform failures

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Transformer<T> {
  name: string
  apply(input: T): Promise<T>
}
class TransformerRegistry<T> {
  private map = new Map<string, Transformer<T>>()
  register(t: Transformer<T>): void { this.map.set(t.name, t) }
  get(name: string): Transformer<T> | undefined { return this.map.get(name) }
}
class Pipeline<T> {
  constructor(private reg: TransformerRegistry<T>, private order: string[]) {}
  async run(input: T): Promise<{out: T; errors: string[]}> {
    let acc = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(input)) as T
    const errors: string[] = []
    for (const name of this.order) {
      const t = this.reg.get(name)
      if (!t) continue
      try { acc = await t.apply(acc) }
      catch (e) { errors.push(name + ': ' + (e?.message ?? 'err')) }
    }
    return { out: acc, errors }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test transformer isolation and error aggregation across multiple records?
- How would you scale this for large streams with backpressure and backoffs?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to combine the Chain of Responsibility with a registry-driven plugin model, enabling runtime composition and reordering of middleware without touching route code.

## Key Concepts

- Chain of Responsibility
- Registry / Plugin architecture
- Dynamic composition and ordering
- Context mutation versus immutability

## Code Example

```typescript
type Context = { [k: string]: any };
type Next = () => Promise<void>;
type Middleware = (ctx: Context, next: Next) => Promise<void>;

class Registry {
  private store = new Map<string, Middleware[]>();
  register(name: string, mw: Middleware) {
    const arr = this.store.get(name) ?? [];
    arr.push(mw); this.store.set(name, arr);
  }
  get(name: string): Middleware[] | undefined {
    return this.store.get(name);
  }
}

function compose(list: Middleware[]): Middleware {
  return async (ctx, next) => {
    let i = -1;
    const dispatch = async (idx: number) => {
      if (idx < 0) return next();
      const mw = list[idx];
      if (!mw) return next();
      await mw(ctx, () => dispatch(idx + 1));
    };
    await dispatch(0);
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle middleware cancellation and timeouts without leaking resources?
- How would you test registry composition and ensure order guarantees across deployments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design runtime extensibility for security-critical components, including hot-reload and safe concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime pluggability via a policy registry
- Evaluation order and conflict resolution
- Thread-safety and atomic swaps for hot-reload
- RBAC/ABAC/PBAC composition and performance trade-offs

## Code Example
```java
interface Policy {
  boolean evaluate(User user, Resource resource, Action action);
  String name();
}
class PolicyRegistry {
  private final java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference<java.util.Map<String, Policy>> policies =
      new java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference<>(java.util.Collections.emptyMap());

  public void register(String name, Policy p) {
    var next = new java.util.HashMap<>(policies.get());
    next.put(name, p);
    policies.set(java.util.Collections.unmodifiableMap(next));
  }

  public boolean evaluate(User user, Resource resource, Action action) {
    for (Policy p : policies.get().values()) {
      if (p.evaluate(user, resource, action)) return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  public void reload(java.util.Map<String, Policy> newPolicies) {
    policies.set(java.util.Collections.unmodifiableMap(new java.util.HashMap<>(newPolicies)));
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you determine policy precedence when multiple policies apply with conflicting results?
- How would you test hot-reload paths without impacting live requests?

